// What asks for a memory in Emberdown (bible 10, docs/design/offerings.md; `Offerings`). The offer command gives it;
// can_offer(asker, memory) and offered(asker) ask. A given memory is gone for good. The ninth chimney's door: nobody
// remembers building it (the Ninth Chimney commission), so it admits only the counted.

title: Ninth_Door
---
<<if offered("ninth_door")>>
    Door: Open. Soot on the sill, and a satchel's shape in the dust where one was. #still #line:0bffeda
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
Door: A door at the ninth's top. No name cut over it. The soot on the stone is fresh. #still #line:0625be1
Door: Scratched at talon height: "Counted, come in. Strangers, the stair." #still #line:0ecc2bb
<<if not can_offer("ninth_door", "kettil.count")>>
    Door: It waits for a count. Not a number: a count, with you in it. #still #line:0a88672
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
-> Forty-two, Wren. Forty-three, whoever's next. #line:017fb5a
    <<offer ninth_door kettil.count>>
    Door: The stone takes the count. Somewhere below, the bell rings for nobody in particular. #line:0f07bc3
    Door: Behind it, the builder's satchel. Two scraps of vellum, and a name, rubbed out. #line:0b61997
-> The count's the town's. Not a door's. #line:07b06b8
    Door: It waits. Nobody remembers building it. It's used to waiting. #still #line:038879f
-> ... #line:0f5a970
    Door: Soot settles. It can wait. #still #line:03e1c26
===
